{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Implementation findings\n\n**1. Two of the six in-scope sites needed no work — the sweep note was stale.**\n\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock.py` is already fully bounded, and structurally so: its single git seam `_git(repo_root, args, *, timeout: float, ...)` (line 244) declares `timeout` as a REQUIRED keyword-only parameter with no default, and holds the module's only `run_git` call. An unbounded git call is therefore unrepresentable in that module. Both named sites — `_remote_ref_absent`'s `ls-remote --exit-code` and `_push_lease_cas`'s `push --force-with-lease` — pass `_REMOTE_TIMEOUT_SECS` (30.0s), and a timeout already raises `RefLockTimeoutError` naming the operation and the bound, fail-closed as HELD. Left unedited, which also honours the standing instruction not to touch `_engine/rebar_reconciler/**`.\n\n**2. Four sites actually fixed, across two modules, at `_GIT_TIMEOUT = 300`.**\n\nAll six `subprocess.run` call sites in the two modules are now bounded (AST-verified, not grep-verified): `gerrit_client.py` lines 331/338/345/360/367 and `workspace.py` line 54.\n\n**3. A security finding the ticket had not anticipated.**\n\nIn `gerrit_client.clone_change_ref`, `subprocess.TimeoutExpired` is neither an `OSError` nor a `CalledProcessError`, so it fell outside the existing `except` tuple — and `TimeoutExpired.cmd` carries the `user:token@host` fetch URL. Simply adding `timeout=` without also routing the new exception through the handler would have converted an unbounded hang into a **credential-disclosure path**. The fix extracts the existing redaction into `GerritClient._redact()` and runs the timeout message through the identical raw + percent-encoded scrub; a regression test asserts neither form of the token appears. The extracted helper also fixes a latent edge: the old inline code did `str.replace(\"\", \"***\")` when the token was empty, which inserts `***` between every character.\n\n**4. Sibling sweep (Step 7) — bounded, and it found nothing further in this class.**\n\nAn AST sweep over all of `src/rebar` for `subprocess.run` / `Popen` / `run_git` / `_git*` helpers lacking a forwarded `timeout`, filtered to network-capable git subcommands, returns exactly the two deferred `_commands/` sites (`init.py:86` `_git`, reached by the `fetch` at :375; `fsck_repair.py:56` `_git`, reached by the `push` at :338) — both already carried on task 983f-ee85-1e06-428e. One further hit, `_engine/rebar_reconciler/git_adapter.py:149` (`git remote get-url`), is a purely local config read and a false positive of the keyword filter.\n\nSeparately noted and deliberately NOT in scope: many module-local `_git` helpers for purely LOCAL git ops are also unbounded (`_commands/compact.py`, `delete.py`, `fsck.py`, `txn.py`, `_store/event_append.py`, `metrics/blame.py`, `metrics/git_metrics.py`). A local git op can still stall on a stuck `index.lock` or a hung credential helper, but that is a materially lower-severity class than the network hang 747f measured, and bounding it repo-wide is a separate decision. Not filed as a ticket — flagged here so the choice is recorded rather than silently made.\n\n## RED -> GREEN evidence\n\nRED (before the fix, test written first and held out from the implementing subagent): 8 failed — `_GIT_TIMEOUT` absent from both modules, `git call(s) invoked with no timeout`, and raw `subprocess.TimeoutExpired` propagating out of both modules.\n\nGREEN (after): 8 passed.\n\nMutation check — four separate re-introductions of the confirmed root cause each returned the suite to RED, so the test is not a tautology:\n- drop `timeout=` from the gerrit fetch -> 1 failed (the bounded-call assertion)\n- delete the gerrit `except TimeoutExpired` -> 2 failed (descriptive error + token leak)\n- make `workspace._git` re-raise `TimeoutExpired` -> 2 failed (direct + via `prepare_workspace`)\n- drop `timeout=` from `workspace._git` -> 1 failed\n\n## The `git archive` question — resolved read-only, NOT a defect\n\n`src/rebar/llm/workflow/snapshot.py` was flagged unjudged: does its `git archive --format=tar <sha>` degrade like the `read-tree`/`checkout-index` pair 747f fixed? **Measured, answer no.** On a 40-file `--filter=blob:none` fixture with `uploadpack.allowFilter` enabled (git 2.55.0), `GIT_TRACE2_EVENT` `child_start` counting shows 40 missing blobs resolved by exactly ONE batched child `git fetch ... --filter=blob:none --stdin` — against 40 separate sequential fetches for the primitives 747f fixed. `git archive` batches its promisor prefetch, so it is not an instance of the 747f bug class. No edit made and no follow-up filed; the file belongs to a parallel session and was read only."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785533574035592001,"uuid":"b45c25f8-60cd-460e-b848-d6e7ba41ab81"}